ed facets remain unassessed.","tree:evidenceAssessment":[{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#assessment-node%3Agraphicalmodels%3Adevelopment"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#assessment-node%3Agraphicalmodels%3AmapStatus"}],"tree:claimFingerprint":"d2887b51"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#node-topicmodels","@type":["schema:DefinedTerm","prov:Entity","tree:ResearchDevelopment"],"schema:identifier":"topicmodels","schema:name":"Topic models (LDA)","schema:description":"Latent Dirichlet allocation modeled corpora as mixtures of themes. Embeddings and LLMs displaced it from the frontier, while interpretable corpus exploration and digital-humanities uses persist.","schema:inDefinedTermSet":{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866"},"schema:url":{"@id":"./#node=topicmodels"},"schema:citation":[{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#work-blei-ng-jordan-lda-2003"}],"tree:recordType":"atlasEntry","tree:startYear":2003,"tree:endYear":2003,"tree:dateLabel":"2003","tree:lane":{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#lane-prob"},"tree:legacyClassification":{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#classification-d"},"tree:legacyClassificationCode":"d","tree:legacyClassificationLabel":"Dormant","tree:kind":"development","tree:significance":"not_assessed","tree:activity":"dormant","tree:trajectory":"not_assessed","tree:statusAsOf":"2026-08-04","tree:statusConfidence":"low","tree:statusRationale":"Low-confidence editorial migration from the legacy category; unrepresented facets remain unassessed.","tree:evidenceAssessment":[{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#assessment-node%3Atopicmodels%3Adevelopment"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#assessment-node%3Atopicmodels%3AmapStatus"}],"tree:landmarkWorkAssociation":[{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#work-link-topicmodels%3Eblei-ng-jordan-lda-2003"}],"tree:claimFingerprint":"04c43856"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#node-recsys","@type":["schema:DefinedTerm","prov:Entity","tree:ResearchDevelopment"],"schema:identifier":"recsys","schema:name":"Recommender systems","schema:description":"Netflix Prize matrix factorization → trillion-parameter ad models. The profitable dark matter of ML — huge, deployed, rarely glamorous.","schema:inDefinedTermSet":{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866"},"schema:url":{"@id":"./#node=recsys"},"tree:recordType":"atlasEntry","tree:startYear":2007,"tree:endYear":2007,"tree:dateLabel":"2007","tree:lane":{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#lane-prob"},"tree:legacyClassification":{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#classification-a"},"tree:legacyClassificationCode":"a","tree:legacyClassificationLabel":"Active","tree:kind":"development","tree:significance":"not_assessed","tree:activity":"active","tree:trajectory":"not_assessed","tree:statusAsOf":"2026-08-04","tree:statusConfidence":"low","tree:statusRationale":"Low-confidence editorial migration from the legacy category; unrepresented facets remain unassessed.","tree:evidenceAssessment":[{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#assessment-node%3Arecsys%3Adevelopment"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#assessment-node%3Arecsys%3AmapStatus"}],"tree:claimFingerprint":"0c5c63e2"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#node-causal","@type":["schema:DefinedTerm","prov:Entity","tree:ResearchDevelopment"],"schema:identifier":"causal","schema:name":"Causal inference","schema:description":"do-calculus and counterfactuals: reasoning about interventions, not correlations. Deep learning's acknowledged blind spot; integration keeps being attempted.","schema:inDefinedTermSet":{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866"},"schema:url":{"@id":"./#node=causal"},"schema:citation":[{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#work-pearl-causality-2009"}],"tree:recordType":"atlasEntry","tree:startYear":2000,"tree:endYear":2000,"tree:dateLabel":"2000","tree:lane":{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#lane-prob"},"tree:legacyClassification":{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#classification-a"},"tree:legacyClassificationCode":"a","tree:legacyClassificationLabel":"Active","tree:kind":"development","tree:significance":"not_assessed","tree:activity":"active","tree:trajectory":"not_assessed","tree:statusAsOf":"2026-08-04","tree:statusConfidence":"low","tree:statusRationale":"Low-confidence editorial migration from the legacy category; unrepresented facets remain unassessed.","tree:evidenceAssessment":[{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#assessment-node%3Acausal%3Adevelopment"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#assessment-node%3Acausal%3AmapStatus"}],"tree:landmarkWorkAssociation":[{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#work-link-causal%3Epearl-causality-2009"}],"tree:claimFingerprint":"f19137f5"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#node-bayesopt","@type":["schema:DefinedTerm","prov:Entity","tree:ResearchDevelopment"],"schema:identifier":"bayesopt","schema:name":"Bayesian optimization, NAS & AutoML","schema:description":"Bayesian optimization tunes expensive black boxes sample-efficiently; neural architecture search and population-based training broadened AutoML into architecture and schedule search. Useful everywhere, fully automatic science nowhere.","schema:inDefinedTermSet":{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866"},"schema:url":{"@id":"./#node=bayesopt"},"schema:citation":[{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#paper-arxiv-1611.01578"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#paper-arxiv-1711.09846"}],"tree:recordType":"atlasEntry","tree:startYear":2012,"tree:endYear":2012,"tree:dateLabel":"2012","tree:lane":{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#lane-prob"},"tree:legacyClassification":{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#classification-a"},"tree:legacyClassificationCode":"a","tree:legacyClassificationLabel":"Active","tree:kind":"development","tree:significance":"not_assessed","tree:activity":"active","tree:trajectory":"not_assessed","tree:statusAsOf":"2026-08-04","tree:statusConfidence":"low","tree:statusRationale":"Low-confidence editorial migration from the legacy category; unrepresented facets remain unassessed.","tree:evidenceAssessment":[{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#assessment-node%3Abayesopt%3Adevelopment"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#assessment-node%3Abayesopt%3AmapStatus"}],"tree:researchTier":"integrated","tree:tag":["AutoML","optimization","search"],"tree:researchQuestion":["When is automatic search more sample-efficient and reproducible than expert-designed architectures and schedules?"],"tree:paperAssociation":[{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#paper-link-bayesopt%3E1611.01578"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#paper-link-bayesopt%3E1711.09846"}],"tree:claimFingerprint":"473abe18"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#node-probprog","@type":["schema:DefinedTerm","prov:Entity","tree:ResearchDevelopment"],"schema:identifier":"probprog","schema:name":"Probabilistic programming","schema:description":"Models expressed as programs with automated inference, including Stan and language-based systems descended from Church. A specialist but active ecosystem used in science, statistics, and uncertainty-aware modeling.","schema:inDefinedTermSet":{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866"},"schema:url":{"@id":"./#node=probprog"},"tree:recordType":"atlasEntry","tree:startYear":2012,"tree:endYear":2012,"tree:dateLabel":"2012","tree:lane":{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#lane-prob"},"tree:legacyClassification":{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#classification-a"},"tree:legacyClassificationCode":"a","tree:legacyClassificationLabel":"Active","tree:kind":"development","tree:significance":"not_assessed","tree:activity":"active","tree:trajectory":"not_assessed","tree:statusAsOf":"2026-08-04","tree:statusConfidence":"low","tree:statusRationale":"Low-confidence editorial migration from the legacy category; unrepresented facets remain unassessed.","tree:evidenceAssessment":[{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#assessment-node%3Aprobprog%3Adevelopment"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#assessment-node%3Aprobprog%3AmapStatus"}],"tree:claimFingerprint":"89a0bc1d"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#node-conformal","@type":["schema:DefinedTerm","prov:Entity","tree:ResearchDevelopment"],"schema:identifier":"conformal","schema:name":"Calibration, ensembles & conformal UQ","schema:description":"Deep ensembles and calibration estimate uncertainty empirically; conformal methods add distribution-free coverage guarantees under stated assumptions. A broader reliability toolkit than any one method — still underused in LLM deployment.","schema:inDefinedTermSet":{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866"},"schema:url":{"@id":"./#node=conformal"},"schema:citation":[{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#paper-arxiv-1612.01474"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#paper-arxiv-1706.04599"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#paper-arxiv-2107.07511"}],"tree:recordType":"atlasEntry","tree:startYear":2016,"tree:endYear":2016,"tree:dateLabel":"2016","tree:lane":{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#lane-prob"},"tree:legacyClassification":{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#classification-a"},"tree:legacyClassificationCode":"a","tree:legacyClassificationLabel":"Active","tree:kind":"development","tree:significance":"not_assessed","tree:activity":"active","tree:trajectory":"not_assessed","tree:statusAsOf":"2026-08-04","tree:statusConfidence":"low","tree:statusRationale":"Low-confidence editorial migration from the legacy category; unrepresented facets remain unassessed.","tree:evidenceAssessment":[{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#assessment-node%3Aconformal%3Adevelopment"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#assessment-node%3Aconformal%3AmapStatus"}],"tree:researchTier":"integrated","tree:tag":["uncertainty","reliability","statistics"],"tree:researchQuestion":["How should calibration, epistemic uncertainty and coverage guarantees be combined under non-exchangeable deployment data?"],"tree:paperAssociation":[{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#paper-link-conformal%3E1612.01474"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#paper-link-conformal%3E1706.04599"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#paper-link-conformal%3E2107.07511"}],"tree:claimFingerprint":"74334407"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#node-gap_causal","@type":["schema:DefinedTerm","prov:Entity","tree:OpenResearchDirection"],"schema:identifier":"gap_causal","schema:name":"Causal world models","schema:description":"Research direction: test whether explicit causal intervention and counterfactual structure improves learned-world-model planning under distribution shift. Partial causal-world systems exist; reliable gains at useful scale and under hidden confounding remain unresolved.","schema:inDefinedTermSet":{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866"},"schema:url":{"@id":"./#node=gap_causal"},"schema:citation":[{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#paper-arxiv-2012.14228"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#paper-arxiv-2510.14049"}],"tree:recordType":"atlasEntry","tree:startYear":2026,"tree:endYear":2026,"tree:dateLabel":"2026","tree:lane":{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#lane-prob"},"tree:legacyClassification":{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#classification-g"},"tree:legacyClassificationCode":"g","tree:legacyClassificationLabel":"Open direction","tree:kind":"open_direction","tree:significance":"not_assessed","tree:activity":"not_assessed","tree:trajectory":"not_assessed","tree:statusAsOf":"2026-08-04","tree:statusConfidence":"low","tree:statusRationale":"Low-confidence editorial migration from the legacy category; unrepresented facets remain unassessed.","tree:evidenceAssessment":[{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#assessment-node%3Agap_causal%3Adevelopment"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#assessment-node%3Agap_causal%3AmapStatus"}],"tree:researchQuestion":["When do explicit intervention and counterfactual structures improve learned-world planning under distribution shift relative to equally capable noncausal world models?"],"tree:paperAssociation":[{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#paper-link-gap_causal%3E2012.14228"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#paper-link-gap_causal%3E2510.14049"}],"tree:claimFingerprint":"30f38bab","tree:directionClaimState":"editorial_hypothesis","tree:directionAsOf":"2026-08-04","tree:directionConfidence":"medium","tree:closureCriteria":"On held-out interventions and mechanism changes, a causal world model improves planning success, calibration, and sample efficiency over matched latent-dynamics and invariant-learning baselines, with gains surviving misspecified graphs and latent confounding.","tree:partialResult":["Causal World Models use unsupervised deconfounding and report improvements in reinforcement-learning sample complexity and counterfactual physical reasoning.","CausalVerse provides high-fidelity configurable simulations with known causal structure and intervention histories.","Existing causal-control systems remain mainly simulated or sensitive to structural assumptions."],"tree:starterProject":["Individual: Reproduce a causal-world-model result in CausalWorld and add hidden-confounder, mechanism-shift, and graph-misspecification stress tests.","Small lab: Compare explicit causal, invariant, and standard latent world models on matched CausalVerse training data and unseen intervention families.","Well-funded lab: Collect controlled real-robot interventions and test whether simulation-learned causal structure improves adaptation to altered objects, actuators, and dynamics."],"tree:counterexample":["Causal World Models explicitly combine learned dynamics, latent deconfounding, counterfactual prediction, and reinforcement learning.","Explainable reinforcement learning via causal world models combines causal structure with control and explanation.","Interventional causal-representation methods learn structure from intervention families in controlled settings."],"tree:crowdedness":"Medium across causal representation learning, world models, and causal reinforcement learning.","tree:tractability":"Medium in configurable simulation and low under real-world latent confounding.","tree:noveltyReview":{"@value":{"status":"targeted_search","checkedAt":"2026-08-04","scope":"arXiv and official repositories for causal world models, causal representation learning, interventions, counterfactual physical reasoning, and causal reinforcement learning.","note":"Partial fusion already exists, so the open question concerns robust benefit and scaling rather than whether any causal world-model system exists."},"@type":"@json"},"tree:researchResources":{"@value":{"benchmarks":["CausalWorld","CausalVerse","Causal Triplet","CALVIN or ManiSkill as downstream external tests"],"code":["https://github.com/rr-learning/CausalWorld","https://github.com/CausalVerse/CausalVerseBenchmark","https://github.com/libo-huang/Awesome-Causal-Reinforcement-Learning"],"datasets":["https://huggingface.co/CausalVerse/datasets","CausalWorld intervention trajectories","Causal Triplet"],"estimated":{"individual":"1 x 24 GB GPU for CausalWorld over 2-5 days","smallLab":"4-8 x 80 GB GPUs for CausalVerse or video experiments over 2-3 weeks","wellFundedLab":"32-64 GPUs plus intervention-capable robots for 1-3 months"}},"@type":"@json"}},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#node-gap_tabular","@type":["schema:DefinedTerm","prov:Entity","tree:OpenResearchDirection"],"schema:identifier":"gap_tabular","schema:name":"Deep learning vs tabular data","schema:description":"Research direction: predict and test the data regimes, shifts, modalities and tuning budgets under which deep tabular models outperform strong boosted-tree ensembles. Deep models already win in some large, multimodal or representation-rich settings; a reliable regime-selection rule remains unresolved.","schema:inDefinedTermSet":{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866"},"schema:url":{"@id":"./#node=gap_tabular"},"schema:citation":[{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#paper-arxiv-2207.08815"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#paper-arxiv-2606.30410"}],"tree:recordType":"atlasEntry","tree:startYear":2026,"tree:endYear":2026,"tree:dateLabel":"2026","tree:lane":{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#lane-prob"},"tree:legacyClassification":{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#classification-g"},"tree:legacyClassificationCode":"g","tree:legacyClassificationLabel":"Open direction","tree:kind":"open_direction","tree:significance":"not_assessed","tree:activity":"not_assessed","tree:trajectory":"not_assessed","tree:statusAsOf":"2026-08-04","tree:statusConfidence":"low","tree:statusRationale":"Low-confidence editorial migration from the legacy category; unrepresented facets remain unassessed.","tree:evidenceAssessment":[{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#assessment-node%3Agap_tabular%3Adevelopment"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#assessment-node%3Agap_tabular%3AmapStatus"}],"tree:researchQuestion":["Under equal tuning and total-cost budgets, which data regimes and distribution shifts reliably favor neural or tabular-foundation models over boosted trees, and which still favor trees?"],"tree:paperAssociation":[{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#paper-link-gap_tabular%3E2207.08815"},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#paper-link-gap_tabular%3E2606.30410"}],"tree:claimFingerprint":"1adb3d06","tree:directionClaimState":"editorial_hypothesis","tree:directionAsOf":"2026-08-04","tree:directionConfidence":"high","tree:closureCriteria":"Produce a leak-checked, preregistered regime map covering IID, temporal, grouped, large, high-dimensional, missing, and high-cardinality data, with equal tuning time and total compute plus latency, calibration, and energy reporting.","tree:partialResult":["The earlier 45-dataset benchmark found tree-based models strongest on roughly 10,000-sample datasets.","TabArena finds deep methods catch up with larger time budgets and ensembles while foundation models excel on smaller datasets.","BeyondArena finds tabular foundation models strongest on tiny-to-medium IID data while trees and conventional deep models dominate several non-IID, large, and high-dimensional regimes."],"tree:starterProject":["Individual: Compare CatBoost, XGBoost, TabPFN, and TabM on 8-12 datasets using both random and temporal splits under an identical one-hour tuning budget.","Small lab: Build a preregistered 100-plus-dataset regime map including latency, energy, calibration, missingness, high-cardinality features, and temporal or group shift.","Well-funded lab: Train a tabular foundation model on disjoint source corpora and evaluate frozen cross-domain and non-IID transfer against the best trees and AutoGluon under total-cost parity."],"tree:counterexample":["TabR beats gradient-boosted decision trees on a benchmark designed to be favorable to them.","TabPFN and later versions lead on several small-to-medium IID suites.","TabArena shows deep ensembles catch up under larger time budgets.","BeyondArena shows that no one family dominates across IID and non-IID regimes."],"tree:crowdedness":"Very high across boosted trees, deep tabular models, foundation models, and AutoML.","tree:tractability":"High for rigorous replication and regime mapping; medium for new architectures.","tree:noveltyReview":{"@value":{"status":"targeted_search","checkedAt":"2026-08-04","scope":"arXiv, living benchmarks, and official repositories for tabular deep learning, foundation models, gradient-boosted trees, temporal and group shift, and equal-budget comparisons.","note":"The old blanket claim that trees beat deep learning is no longer supportable; confidence is high only for the revised regime-selection and non-IID robustness question."},"@type":"@json"},"tree:researchResources":{"@value":{"benchmarks":["TabArena","BeyondArena","TabReD","OpenML-CC18","Grinsztajn-45"],"code":["https://github.com/autogluon/tabarena","https://github.com/PriorLabs/TabPFN","https://github.com/yandex-research/tabred","https://github.com/yandex-research/tabular-dl-tabr","https://docs.openml.org/benchmark/"],"datasets":["TabArena curated datasets","BeyondArena 142-dataset collection","TabReD temporal industry datasets","OpenML-CC18"],"estimated":{"individual":"CPU plus 1 x 24 GB GPU for a subset over 2-7 days","smallLab":"4-8 GPUs and 32-64 CPU cores for full hyperparameter optimization over 1-3 weeks","wellFundedLab":"32 or more GPUs plus a large CPU fleet for a living benchmark or foundation-model training"}},"@type":"@json"}},{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866#node-cellauto","@type":["schema:DefinedTerm","prov:Entity","tree:ResearchDevelopment"],"schema:identifier":"cellauto","schema:name":"Self-reproducing automata","schema:description":"Von Neumann's cellular automata: machines that copy themselves. The origin of artificial life and complexity science.","schema:inDefinedTermSet":{"@id":"urn:uuid:7d0547f2-6239-5a56-82a3-1c846701c866"},"sc